1
00:00:03,664 --> 00:00:10,684
This continues the cabin from tutorial 2,
so the units are set. Let's dress it up.

2
00:00:10,734 --> 00:00:15,114
On the Materials tab, open the Material Manager.

3
00:00:15,164 --> 00:00:21,229
Each material is now a little sphere
wearing it, rendered with the real shaders.

4
00:00:21,279 --> 00:00:29,739
The Model tab holds this model's materials.
The Library tab holds ready-made ones.

5
00:00:29,789 --> 00:00:45,175
Plain colors first, then vegetation, then
construction: brick, tiles, stone, glass, metal.

6
00:00:45,225 --> 00:00:49,180
Select the four walls.

7
00:00:49,230 --> 00:00:56,330
Pick Red brick, then Apply to Selection. That
adds it to the model and paints the walls.

8
00:00:56,380 --> 00:01:02,360
Ooh, real bricks. The tick marks
library materials already in the model.

9
00:01:02,410 --> 00:01:09,910
Now a texture for the roof. Back on
the Model tab, choose New Material.

10
00:01:09,960 --> 00:01:26,483
Name it, then give it an image. Browse opens
a file dialog; typing the path works too.

11
00:01:26,533 --> 00:01:36,792
The image covers 4 by 4 units.
That's 1.2 metres in this model.

12
00:01:36,842 --> 00:01:49,552
Select the roof, then Apply to Selection.

13
00:01:55,124 --> 00:02:03,344
Hmm, the boards are narrow. Choose Edit
Texture Position, then click the roof.

14
00:02:03,394 --> 00:02:09,294
Drag a corner to size the image.
The opposite corner stays put.

15
00:02:09,344 --> 00:02:17,221
Nice, wider boards. The drag is saved
to the material, and Undo takes it back.

16
00:02:17,271 --> 00:02:24,131
Next, something shiny on the lawn.
Press T on the grid for a helper.

17
00:02:24,181 --> 00:02:38,539
Sphere: pick a centre up the helper,
then a point above it for the radius.

18
00:02:38,589 --> 00:02:46,576
New Material now lists every shader
in one place. Open the Program list.

19
00:02:46,626 --> 00:02:58,190
Emboss tiling, rough stone, metal, glass
and Perlin noise. Pick Polished metal.

20
00:02:58,240 --> 00:03:06,206
The sphere is still selected, so Apply to Selection.

21
00:03:06,256 --> 00:03:13,916
Look, a mirror. Metal reflects a
studio sky with hard highlights.

22
00:03:13,966 --> 00:03:19,626
Shader Programs lists every program and what uses it.

23
00:03:19,676 --> 00:03:25,576
Deleting a custom program turns
its materials back into plain ones.

24
00:03:25,626 --> 00:03:32,096
New Shader opens the shader editor. Full
Source shows exactly what gets compiled.

25
00:03:32,146 --> 00:03:37,166
No hidden layers. We won't write one today, so Cancel.

26
00:03:37,216 --> 00:03:50,440
Last, the lawn. Rectangle, and click
two grid points around the cabin.

27
00:03:50,490 --> 00:04:05,360
In the Library, double-click Grass.
It joins the model with a tick.

28
00:04:09,807 --> 00:04:15,793
Then Apply to Selection.

29
00:04:15,843 --> 00:04:28,461
Wooow. Every material is saved with
the project, and stays editable.

30
00:04:28,511 --> 00:04:39,142
And the Model tab now shows them
all: brick, decking, metal and grass.
